WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.696 --> 00:00:04.280
(drumming)

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.780
- For centuries, my ancestors have been

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.500
the callers of powerful elements.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:14.193
(singing in foreign language)

00:00:15.123 --> 00:00:19.230
The rain, the fog, the wind,
and the storms of our land.

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:23.420
They did this through hula,

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:26.030
and through a very strong belief system

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:27.820
that when we call they'll come.

00:00:35.687 --> 00:00:38.510
(peaceful music)
(rain falling)

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:42.640
Waimea is like no other
town in the state of Hawaii.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.870
We don't have any coconut
trees in town. (laughing)

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:50.820
We're the closest town to Mauna Kea,

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.640
and so we get a lot of
rain that settles here.

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:58.920
We truly are the people of the clouds.

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:02.290
Our children are named after the fog.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:05.040
Our children are named after the rain.

00:01:08.361 --> 00:01:10.611
(drumming)

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.380
We are the halau for Waimea.

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:19.370
We are known to be able to
call the elements when needed.

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:23.743
Rain, fog, wind, the storms of our land.

00:01:23.743 --> 00:01:27.720
And so our dances mimic
the whipping of the wind

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.726
and the pounding of the rain.

00:01:29.726 --> 00:01:31.660
(yelling)

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:34.980
On the outside it looks like
it's just entertainment.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.380
We are not a halau that entertains.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.870
You won't see us down at the hotels.

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:43.190
You are watching the
physical manifestation

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:45.444
of the spiritual world that we inhabit.

00:01:45.444 --> 00:01:49.506
(yelling in foreign language)

00:01:49.506 --> 00:01:51.950
(rapid music)

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.980
We are completely woven
into our community.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.840
When there's not enough
snow on the mountain,

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:59.890
we get calls from astronomers
to help call the snow.

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:04.780
In times of drought, we're often called

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:07.740
to chant into the heavens and ask the rain

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.590
to feed and nourish the land.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:12.910
You know, every time we call them they come.

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:15.750
But they don't just come
because you call them.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.540
Your inside has to really pull them.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:20.360
When you're finished
with all of your chants,

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:21.960
you should be drenched in sweat.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.670
It makes it so much more powerful

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:28.680
to be wearing what your ancestors wore.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.030
They wore what we call kapa.

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:33.889
It comes from a plant that we call wauke.

00:02:33.889 --> 00:02:35.470
(clipping)

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:38.370
It's gathered, the bark is stripped off,

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:40.451
it's soaked and then pounded.

00:02:40.451 --> 00:02:41.930
(wood pounding)

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:43.580
You go through weeks and even months

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:45.130
of pounding and pounding.

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:47.870
You want the fabric to
come out like soft cotton.

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:49.200
And when we're out pounding kapa,

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.547
you can hear it about
two miles down the road.

00:02:51.547 --> 00:02:53.110
(wood pounding)

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:55.180
It transports them into the realm

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:56.970
of what our ancestors had to do.

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:00.350
To be in a halau, you need to go through

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:02.660
the entire process of the kapa.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:04.460
Without your skirt, you can't dance.

00:03:07.788 --> 00:03:10.538
(peaceful music)

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.720
It's the pinnacle of
the hula dancer's career

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:17.800
to be able to dance in the forest,

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.410
amongst the most supreme beings.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.210
We'll hike into the back of the forest

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:26.540
or on the top of the mountain.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:29.301
And no one's there, just us.

00:03:29.301 --> 00:03:31.540
And we dance for the only
audience that matters,

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:32.870
which is our ancestors.

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:39.120
They're in the form of the
plants that are around us,

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.730
the hills that surround us,

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:44.850
the wind and the rain that
comes and caresses us.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:48.640
After we have danced, we don't

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.460
really remember what happened.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:53.495
We just remember that we
were in complete bliss.

00:03:53.495 --> 00:03:56.245
(peaceful music)

